# optdiff.sh skiplist — programs whose output is legitimately nondeterministic
# (never a miscompile signal). Shell-glob patterns, one per line, matched
# against the basename. Keep this list visible and short; a new entry needs a
# one-line reason.
test_c_gtk_*.c
test_c_gtk_*.pas
# thread interleaving: output order differs run to run even at -O0
test_multithreading*
# prints RTTI/prop POINTER values — binary-layout-dependent, differs
# between binaries by construction
test_rtti*
# SAME CAUSE AS t_rw* BELOW, AND IT REACHED optdiff BY A SPELLING: the two
# patterns above are `test_c_gtk_*`, which does NOT match `test_c_gtk3_stock`
# -- gtk3, no underscore. This is optdiff#shard5/12, red since 2026-09-07 and
# the longest-standing of the shard reds. Verified before skipping, as this
# list requires, and the criterion is the PROGRAM'S BEHAVIOUR rather than its
# name: with no display reachable it exits 1 at every level (which is the shard
# log's own `rc 1 vs 1`) and prints
#   (d0:654383): Gtk-WARNING **: 18:44:48.983: cannot open display:
# whose THREE varying fields are the binary name -- which optdiff itself varies
# by compiling to d0/d2/d3 -- the PID, and a millisecond timestamp. It cannot
# compare equal at any level, ever, for three independent reasons.
# NOT nondeterministic in the shard9 sense: 10 runs are byte-identical, and
# WITH a display reachable both levels exit 0 and agree, which is why a
# standalone re-run reports diff=0 and refutes nothing. The other two uncovered
# gtk sources were measured too and are NOT affected (both exit 0 and agree
# with no display -- they never open one), so this entry is deliberately ONE
# file and not a widened gtk3 glob. bug: none -- the program is correct and the
# instrument's question is meaningless here.
test_c_gtk3_stock*
# a GTK3 GUI program: with no display gtk_init prints a warning carrying the
# BINARY NAME, the PID and a millisecond timestamp — and optdiff compiles the
# three levels to d0/d2/d3, so the first of those differs by construction.
# Dormant until 74702c14d stopped jobs inheriting the human's $DISPLAY: before
# that it opened a real window on the workstation session and was skipped as
# TIMEOUT-O0, so the sweep never compared it.
t_rw*
# ---- THE ATOMICITY FAMILY: three programs, one reason -------------------
# Several threads each write whole lines with no ordering between them. The
# ORDER is nondeterministic BY DESIGN -- each program's own header says so --
# so raw stdout comparison reports a DIFF at every level, for every pair,
# forever. The property they assert is ATOMICITY: no line is torn. That is
# what their Makefile rows ask, by COUNTING well-formed lines rather than
# comparing bytes, and it is a question optdiff has no way to express.
#
# Verified before skipping, at -O0/-O1/-O2/-O3, five runs each, rather than
# assumed: every level gives the right count every time while three runs of a
# SINGLE binary give three different byte streams. Skipped because the question
# is wrong here, not because the answer is inconvenient.
#
# The pattern to recognise before adding a fourth: several writers, no
# ordering, and a Makefile row that GREPS rather than compares. If a program's
# row compares whole output, it does not belong in this family and a DIFF from
# it is real.
#   thread_writeln_interleave  6 runs of one -O0 binary -> 5 distinct outputs
#   io_lock_foreign            4 foreign threads x 50 lines -> 200 lines + `done`
#   parallel_writeln_atomic    200 parallel-for workers  -> PARWROK + 200 + 200
#
# All three were INVISIBLE until 2026-09-01: they did not build under this
# sweep without --threadsafe, so they were counted as BUILD-FAIL skips. The
# retry that made them build is what turned them up, all three at once, which
# is itself the argument that they are one family and not three accidents.
test_thread_writeln_interleave*
test_threadsafe_io_lock_foreign*
test_parallel_writeln_atomic*
# a KNOWN-FAILING repro, deliberately NOT WIRED into the suite by its own
# ticket (bug-a-the-exception-chain-fix-is-defeated-by-a-libc-pthread, p70):
# it raises 300k exceptions on a raw pthread_create'd FOREIGN thread, which
# inherits its creator's exception chain. optdiff globs test/*.pas, so a
# program the suite deliberately excludes is swept anyway -- the glob's
# population is "files in test/", not "tests". Verified before skipping, as
# this list requires: 20 runs at -O0, 20 at -O2 and 16 under the PINNED v410
# compiler give FOUR distinct exit codes -- 0, 124, 139 (SIGSEGV) and 217 --
# at a FIXED level. One optdiff run reported `217 vs 0` at -O2 and `217 vs
# 139` at -O3, three codes in one sweep. A one-run-per-level differential
# cannot express a question about a program whose exit code is a coin flip.
# The BUG stays open and owned at p70; this skips the INSTRUMENT, not the bug.
test_foreign_thread_exception_chain*
